# Synapse dashboard environment configuration.
#
# Copy to `.env.local` for local development:
#     cp .env.example .env.local
#
# Every value here is `NEXT_PUBLIC_*`, which means it is inlined into the shipped
# browser bundle. NEVER put a secret, private key, or credential in this file.
#
# Configuration is validated by `lib/config/env.ts` and checked during
# `next build`, so a malformed or inconsistent value fails the build with an
# actionable message instead of surfacing as a deployed dashboard reading the
# wrong chain. See "Validation" below.

# ── Target network ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# One of: testnet | futurenet | local
# Defaults to testnet. The network decides the RPC endpoint, the network
# passphrase, and the contract-explorer links.
NEXT_PUBLIC_NETWORK=testnet

# ── RPC endpoint (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Leave unset to use the selected network's canonical public endpoint. Only set
# this to override it, e.g. to point at your own node or a third-party provider.
#
# When set, the host must match the selected network's canonical host, which
# catches the failure mode where a futurenet build silently reads testnet data.
# For NEXT_PUBLIC_NETWORK=local any endpoint is accepted.
#
#   testnet:   https://soroban-testnet.stellar.org
#   futurenet: https://rpc-futurenet.stellar.org
#   local:     http://localhost:8000
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SOROBAN_RPC_URL=

# ── Contract (required for a deployed build) ──────────────────────────────────
# A Stellar contract id: 'C' followed by 55 uppercase base32 characters.
# Leave unset locally to use the mock-data fallback in `lib/mock-data.ts`.
#
# Required when the build advertises a public origin (NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL or
# VERCEL_URL), because a deployed dashboard with no contract silently renders
# mock data. A plain `next build` with no environment set is not treated as a
# deployment, so CI still builds cleanly.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CONTRACT_ID=CAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA

# ── Public origin (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The deployed address, used for OpenGraph/Twitter metadata. If unset, VERCEL_URL
# is used on Vercel, and http://localhost:3000 otherwise.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://synapse.example.com
# VERCEL_URL=               # injected automatically by Vercel; do not set by hand

# ── Validation ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A `NEXT_PUBLIC_SOROBAN_RPC_URL` whose host disagrees with
# NEXT_PUBLIC_NETWORK is rejected, because the dashboard would otherwise render
# correctly while showing another chain's data.
#
# A malformed contract id is rejected, including the common trailing-space
# mistake, and must not be a 'G' account address.
#
# All problems are reported together rather than one per rebuild.
#
# Run the checks yourself:
#     npm run build     # validates as part of the build
#     npm test          # unit tests for the config layer

# ── Per-environment templates ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Each of these is a complete, runnable starting point. Copy one over your local
# env file rather than assembling values by hand:
#
#     cp .env.development.example .env.local    # local dev, mock data
#     cp .env.staging.example     .env.local     # futurenet, contract required
#     cp .env.production.example  .env.local     # testnet, contract required
#
# ── Precedence ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Two layers, applied in this order. Later wins.
#
# 1. Next.js file loading, for local development. Highest priority first:
#
#      .env.development.local      (all envs except test)
#      .env.local                  (all envs except test)
#      .env.development            (development only)
#      .env                        (all envs)
#
#    Plus the parallel `.env.production.*` and `.env.test.*` files, which apply
#    only when NODE_ENV matches. Variables already present in the shell are
#    never overridden by a file, so `NEXT_PUBLIC_NETWORK=futurenet npm run build`
#    wins over anything written in a `.env` file.
#
# 2. Platform environment variables, at build time. These replace the process
#    environment for server-side reads, so a value set in the Vercel dashboard
#    or CI secret store takes precedence over a committed file. This is the layer
#    staging and production should use — see `NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL` and the note
#    on `VERCEL_URL` above.
#
# A deployment therefore needs no `.env` file committed at all: set the variables
# on the platform. The committed files exist to document and to support local
# development, not to carry deployment values.
#
# Note: Next.js only auto-loads files named for `development`, `production`, and
# `test`. `.env.staging` is not a name it recognises, which is why the staging
# template is copied to `.env.local` locally and, on a real deployment, supplied
# through the platform's environment instead.
